---
/*
 * Trust and security: the page a security reviewer reads first. Every
 * statement here comes from the documentation site (fips-and-stig, releases,
 * audit-logging, licensing and each product's security and accessibility
 * pages) or from this repository; nothing is claimed that they do not state.
 * Prerendered like the other marketing pages.
 */
import { services } from '../data/pricing';
import { products } from '../data/products';
import { docsUrl } from '../data/site';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { formatRange } from '../lib/pricing';

const sections = [
    { id: 'build', label: 'How they are built' },
    { id: 'releases', label: 'Release verification' },
    { id: 'audit', label: 'Audit trail' },
    { id: 'sign-in', label: 'Sign-in and sessions' },
    { id: 'data', label: 'Data handling' },
    { id: 'fips', label: 'FIPS and STIG' },
    { id: 'accessibility', label: 'Accessibility' },
    { id: 'testing', label: 'Independent testing' },
    { id: 'disclosure', label: 'Vulnerability disclosure' },
];

const ato = services.ato;
const security = 'info@ens-solutions.com';
---
<BaseLayout
    title="Trust and security"
    description="How ENS Solutions builds, signs and ships ReadyRoom, CertAlert, RSA Operations Portal and Directory Services Portal: supply chain, release verification, audit trail, sign-in controls, data handling, FIPS 140-3 and DISA STIG position, accessibility, and how to report a vulnerability."
>
<section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">Trust and security</p>
        <h1>What a security reviewer needs to know.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
            Four self-hosted applications, one platform baseline. This page collects what is true of
            all of them: how they are built and scanned, how a release is signed and checked, what the
            audit trail records, how people sign in, where data goes, where we stand on FIPS 140-3,
            DISA STIG and Section 508, and how to tell us about a vulnerability. Each statement links
            to the documentation it comes from.
        </p>
        <div class="btn-row">
            <a class="btn btn--primary" href="#disclosure">Report a vulnerability</a>
            <a class="btn btn--ghost" href={docsUrl('fips-and-stig/')}>FIPS and STIG documentation</a>
            <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/.well-known/security.txt">security.txt</a>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<nav class="page-nav" aria-label="Trust page sections">
    <div class="wrap">
        <ul>
            {sections.map((s) => <li><a href={`#${s.id}`}>{s.label}</a></li>)}
        </ul>
    </div>
</nav>

<section class="section" id="build" aria-labelledby="build-heading">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h2 id="build-heading">How the products are built</h2>
        <p class="lede">
            Spring Boot applications on Red Hat UBI 9 images, with no cryptography of their own and a
            build that fails on a fixable vulnerability.
        </p>
        <div class="feature-grid">
            <div class="feature">
                <h3>Runtime</h3>
                <p>
                    Spring Boot on Red Hat OpenJDK 21 (the RSA Operations Portal on OpenJDK 17), in
                    containers built on Red Hat UBI 9 (<code>ubi9/openjdk-21-runtime</code>; the
                    Directory Services Portal&rsquo;s web tier on <code>ubi9/nginx-126</code>). No
                    application&rsquo;s own code implements cryptography: TLS, certificate and revocation
                    checking, hashing, random numbers and the license check go through the Java
                    runtime&rsquo;s providers.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="feature">
                <h3>Vulnerability scanning</h3>
                <p>
                    Every image is scanned with Trivy in CI, and the build fails on a HIGH or CRITICAL
                    vulnerability that has a fix. Accepted risks are listed in each repository&rsquo;s
                    <code>.trivyignore</code> with a reason and a review date.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="feature">
                <h3>Pinned builds</h3>
                <p>
                    The Directory Services Portal&rsquo;s security page documents its pipeline in full:
                    every GitHub Action pinned to a commit and every base image to a digest, Dependabot
                    updates weekly, and CodeQL, <code>npm audit</code> and Trivy on every push and
                    pull request. The other products publish the same supply-chain table on the
                    <a href={docsUrl('fips-and-stig/#supply-chain')}>FIPS and STIG page</a>.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="feature">
                <h3>Hardened by default</h3>
                <p>
                    Security headers and CSRF protection are always on. The Helm charts run every
                    application as non-root with a read-only root file system and no capabilities, and
                    Prometheus metrics are served on a separate management port that stays off every
                    ingress.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="section-more">
            <a href={docsUrl('fips-and-stig/#supply-chain')}>Supply chain, per product, on the documentation site &rarr;</a>
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="releases" aria-labelledby="releases-heading">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h2 id="releases-heading">Release verification</h2>
        <p class="lede">
            A release is a signed deployment bundle and a Helm chart, with an SPDX software bill of
            materials for every image, and you can check all of it before you install.
        </p>
        <div class="split">
            <div>
                <h3>What a release holds</h3>
                <ul class="ticks">
                    <li>The deployment bundle: everything a Docker or Podman Compose installation needs, container images included, so a site with no internet access installs from it alone</li>
                    <li>The Helm chart at the same version, also published at <code>oci://ghcr.io/winllc/charts/&lt;chart&gt;</code></li>
                    <li><code>sbom-*.spdx.json</code>: an SPDX software bill of materials for each container image</li>
                    <li><code>SHA256SUMS</code>, and <code>SHA256SUMS.asc</code>, ENS Solutions&rsquo; GPG signature over the checksums</li>
                </ul>
                <h3>How it is signed</h3>
                <p>
                    On a release tag, CI signs each published image and the Helm chart with cosign:
                    keyless signatures (Sigstore), made with the release workflow&rsquo;s GitHub identity,
                    always by digest. Each image also gets its SPDX SBOM attached as a signed attestation.
                    The checksum file is signed with our GPG release key, which is kept offline and is
                    separate from the key that signs license files. <code>SHA256SUMS.asc</code> is
                    present when the release was signed with it.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div>
                <h3>How to verify a download</h3>
<pre><code># 1. The signature: proves SHA256SUMS came from ENS Solutions
gpg --import ens-solutions-release.asc
gpg --verify SHA256SUMS.asc SHA256SUMS

# 2. The checksums: proves each file matches what was signed
sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS --ignore-missing</code></pre>
                <p class="small muted">
                    Both must succeed. The Downloads page in your account links to our public key when
                    the portal is configured with it. Check the key&rsquo;s fingerprint against the one
                    ENS Solutions gives you separately, never against anything in the download itself.
                </p>
                <h3>How to verify an image</h3>
<pre><code>cosign verify ghcr.io/winllc/in-n-out-work@&lt;digest&gt; \
  --certificate-identity-regexp 'https://github.com/winllc/in-n-out-work/.github/workflows/.*' \
  --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com
cosign verify-attestation --type spdxjson ghcr.io/winllc/in-n-out-work@&lt;digest&gt; \
  --certificate-identity-regexp 'https://github.com/winllc/in-n-out-work/.github/workflows/.*' \
  --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com</code></pre>
                <p class="small muted">
                    That is ReadyRoom; the image names and repositories for the other products are in the
                    documentation. cosign contacts Sigstore&rsquo;s public services to verify a keyless
                    signature, so on an air-gapped network verify on a connected machine before you mirror
                    the images.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="section-more">
            <a href={docsUrl('releases/')}>Releases and downloads: files, verification and installing &rarr;</a>
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="audit" aria-labelledby="audit-heading">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h2 id="audit-heading">Audit trail</h2>
        <p class="lede">
            Every application, and the licensing portal, keeps the same tamper-evident audit trail:
            one event format, <code>ens-audit/1</code>, one set of settings, one Splunk add-on.
        </p>
        <div class="feature-grid">
            <div class="feature">
                <h3>What is recorded</h3>
                <p>
                    Sign-ins (success and failure, with the method), every refused request with its
                    reason, changes to users, roles, settings and data with old and new values, the
                    license, scheduled jobs, and every export. Secrets are never recorded: passwords,
                    tokens, keys, license files, session ids, one-time codes and PINs appear as
                    <code>[redacted]</code>.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="feature">
                <h3>Hash-chained</h3>
                <p>
                    Each event&rsquo;s SHA-256 hash covers the previous event&rsquo;s hash and its own
                    JSON, so changing, removing or inserting an event breaks the chain from that point.
                    The application&rsquo;s <strong>Verify integrity</strong> button reports where, and
                    an auditor can check an export independently with a script from the documentation
                    repository, without access to the application.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="feature">
                <h3>Into your SIEM</h3>
                <p>
                    Splunk HTTP Event Collector, syslog (RFC 5424 over UDP, TCP or TLS), an audit file or
                    the container log for a forwarder, or a pull API with read-only tokens. HEC and
                    syslog delivery is stored first and retried: an unreachable receiver queues events,
                    never slows a request, and raises an alert after 15 minutes of failure.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="feature">
                <h3>Always available</h3>
                <p>
                    The audit page and the pull API work while an application is read-only or
                    unlicensed. Nothing is removed unless you set a retention period, and then only
                    events every enabled output has delivered, with the removal itself audited.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="section-more">
            <a href={docsUrl('audit-logging/')}>Audit logging: the event format, settings and Splunk setup &rarr;</a>
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="sign-in" aria-labelledby="sign-in-heading">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h2 id="sign-in-heading">Sign-in and session controls</h2>
        <p class="lede">
            Smart cards, directory passwords or your identity provider, behind the DoD notice-and-consent
            banner and session limits, all of it audited.
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
            <table>
                <caption class="visually-hidden">Sign-in and session controls across the products</caption>
                <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Control</th>
                    <th scope="col">What the products do</th>
                </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row">Client certificates</th>
                    <td>
                        X.509 client certificates (smart cards such as CAC/PIV) sign in to CertAlert, the
                        Directory Services Portal and ReadyRoom, with OCSP and CRL revocation checking
                        (<code>ENS_X509_REVOCATION</code>) and a list of allowed issuers. Password sign-in can
                        be turned off where only certificate sign-in is allowed. The RSA Operations Portal
                        signs its users in with their Authentication Manager user ID and SecurID passcode.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row">Single sign-on</th>
                    <td>
                        OpenID Connect or SAML 2.0 against your identity provider in every product; for the
                        RSA Operations Portal, operators can start with single sign-on and then enter the
                        SecurID passcode of the same user. Signing out can end the provider&rsquo;s session
                        too (OpenID Connect end-session; SAML single logout both ways, with signed
                        messages): each product&rsquo;s single sign-on page says which release carries it.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row">Notice and consent</th>
                    <td>
                        With <code>ENS_CONSENT_BANNER_ENABLED=true</code> the DoD notice-and-consent banner
                        comes before any sign-in of any kind, enforced by the server, not just shown, and
                        each acknowledgement is audited. The standard DoD text is the default; a site can
                        supply its own.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row">Session limits</th>
                    <td>
                        An idle timeout (30 minutes by default), a shorter one for administrators, an absolute
                        lifetime (12 hours by default) and an optional limit on concurrent sessions per user
                        (<code>ENS_SESSION_*</code>). A session that ends is audited with its reason:
                        idle, absolute or concurrent.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row">Failed sign-ins</th>
                    <td>
                        A password sign-in is a bind as the user, so your directory&rsquo;s lockout policy
                        applies; the Directory Services Portal adds its own lockout, and the RSA Operations
                        Portal adds rate limits and backoff to Authentication Manager&rsquo;s policy.
                        Repeated failures are audited.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row">Browser protections</th>
                    <td>
                        A Content-Security-Policy with no inline scripts, <code>X-Frame-Options: DENY</code>,
                        <code>X-Content-Type-Options</code>, <code>Referrer-Policy</code>,
                        <code>Permissions-Policy</code> and HSTS on TLS; CSRF protection on every
                        state-changing request; <code>HttpOnly</code>, <code>SameSite</code> session cookies,
                        <code>Secure</code> behind a TLS proxy.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <ul class="resource-list resource-list--spaced">
            {products.map((p) => (
                <li><a href={docsUrl(`${p.docsPath}/security/`)}>{p.name} security</a><span>Sign-in, roles, headers and outbound connections</span></li>
            ))}
        </ul>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="data" aria-labelledby="data-heading">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h2 id="data-heading">Data handling</h2>
        <p class="lede">
            You run the software; we never see your data.
        </p>
        <div class="feature-grid feature-grid--pairs">
            <div class="feature">
                <h3>Self-hosted, no route to us needed</h3>
                <p>
                    Every product installs from its release package (Docker or Podman Compose) or Helm chart
                    on your own infrastructure. The license is checked offline, against public keys built
                    into the application, so it works on an air-gapped network. The outbound connections a
                    product makes are listed on its security page, and all of them go to systems you name:
                    your directory, mail server, PKI, SIEM, identity provider and webhook receivers. There is
                    no usage reporting to ENS Solutions.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="feature">
                <h3>The license file</h3>
                <p>
                    A signed plain-text file. It is not secret: it holds only the licensee and what is
                    licensed, can optionally be bound to one installation id, and is verified by the
                    application with no network call. A license signed with ECDSA P-256 as well as Ed25519
                    verifies on a Java runtime in FIPS mode.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="feature">
                <h3>The licensing portal</h3>
                <p>
                    Your account holds your email address, name, organization, license files and download
                    history, and nothing else about your deployment. Passwords are stored with scrypt;
                    session, confirmation and reset tokens only as hashes. Everything that creates or changes
                    a license is in the portal&rsquo;s own hash-chained audit trail, and you see your own
                    account&rsquo;s events on your account page. Release files never pass through the
                    portal: a download is a short-lived link to private storage.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="feature">
                <h3>Personal data in ReadyRoom</h3>
                <p>
                    ReadyRoom records when people sign in to, lock, unlock and sign out of their
                    workstations. Its <a href={docsUrl('readyroom/privacy/')}>privacy page</a> sets out, for
                    a DPIA, a works council or a privacy review, what is stored, where, who can see it, how
                    long it is kept, and how it is exported and erased.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="section-more">
            <a href={docsUrl('licensing/')}>Licensing: the file, binding and what happens offline &rarr;</a>
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="fips" aria-labelledby="fips-heading">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h2 id="fips-heading">FIPS 140-3 and DISA STIG</h2>
        <div class="callout">
            <p>
                <strong>No ENS validation.</strong> ENS Solutions does not supply or claim a FIPS 140-3
                validated cryptographic module, and none of the applications has been validated or
                certified. Whether a deployment meets FIPS 140-3 depends on the Java runtime, the operating
                system and their validated modules, which you choose and configure.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="split">
            <div>
                <h3>Running in FIPS mode</h3>
                <p>
                    Run the images on a RHEL host with FIPS mode enabled, or on an OpenShift cluster
                    installed in FIPS mode. Red Hat&rsquo;s OpenJDK in the container follows the host&rsquo;s
                    FIPS flag and switches to its FIPS providers; the applications need no setting of their
                    own. Red Hat&rsquo;s OpenJDK in FIPS mode has no Ed25519, so there the license&rsquo;s
                    ECDSA P-256 co-signature is the one that counts: a license issued before we enabled P-256
                    signing needs re-signing first, which we do on request. No application has a CI job that
                    runs it end to end on a Java runtime in FIPS mode; the license library does.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div>
                <h3>DISA STIG settings</h3>
                <p>
                    All four applications implement the same platform baseline: the notice-and-consent
                    banner, session limits, security headers, revocation checking where certificates sign
                    in, the hash-chained audit trail forwarded to a SIEM, metrics on a separate management
                    port, and non-root, read-only containers. The settings that need a decision for a STIG
                    deployment, and the options that are not FIPS-appropriate, are documented per product.
                    A STIG-hardened image and the documentation package for your authorization is a
                    service we price at {formatRange({ low: ato.low!, high: ato.high! })} {ato.per}.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <ul class="resource-list resource-list--spaced">
            <li><a href={docsUrl('fips-and-stig/')}>FIPS 140-3 and DISA STIG, suite-wide</a><span>Where the cryptography comes from, TLS defaults, the STIG control table</span></li>
            {products.map((p) => (
                <li><a href={docsUrl(`${p.docsPath}/fips-and-stig/`)}>{p.name}</a><span>Its FIPS notes and STIG settings</span></li>
            ))}
        </ul>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="accessibility" aria-labelledby="accessibility-heading">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h2 id="accessibility-heading">Accessibility</h2>
        <p class="lede">
            WCAG 2.1 level AA is the target, checked on every build; the conformance reports are drafts,
            available on request.
        </p>
        <div class="split">
            <div>
                <p>
                    Every build runs axe-core against each product&rsquo;s pages and fails on a serious or
                    critical violation of the WCAG A and AA rules. The pages offer a skip link, landmarks,
                    one level-one heading per page, text and controls at WCAG contrast, keyboard operation
                    of every control, and status messages for counts, job state and form errors.
                </p>
                <p>
                    None of the products yet claims conformance. For each there is a self-audit against
                    WCAG 2.1 AA and a <strong>draft</strong> Accessibility Conformance Report in the ITI
                    VPAT 2.5 format, kept in the product&rsquo;s source repository for ENS Solutions to review
                    before publication. Ask us for the current copies when a procurement needs them.
                </p>
            </div>
            <ul class="resource-list">
                {products.map((p) => (
                    <li><a href={docsUrl(`${p.docsPath}/accessibility/`)}>{p.name}</a><span>How it is checked, known limits, conformance documents</span></li>
                ))}
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="testing" aria-labelledby="testing-heading">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h2 id="testing-heading">Independent testing</h2>
        <p class="lede">
            We have not yet published a third-party penetration test report for any of the products.
        </p>
        <p>
            What runs on every build is our own: each product&rsquo;s test suite, the Trivy scan of each
            image and the accessibility checks (see <a href="#build">how the products are built</a>). The
            outputs a reviewer can
            take away today are the SBOMs, the cosign signatures and attestations on the images, and the
            documentation site, which was written from the source code. Requests for test evidence,
            questionnaire answers or a review call with an engineer go through the
            <a href="/contact.html">contact page</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
            The <strong>compliance documentation pack</strong> &mdash; the NIST SP 800-53 control matrix for
            each product, the software bills of materials, and the vulnerability management, secure
            development and supply chain documents &mdash; is at
            <a href="/account/compliance">/account/compliance</a> for accounts ENS has granted access;
            <a href="/contact.html?product=compliance">request it</a> with the product you are reviewing.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="disclosure" aria-labelledby="disclosure-heading">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h2 id="disclosure-heading">Vulnerability disclosure</h2>
        <p class="lede">
            If you believe you have found a security problem in one of our products, the licensing
            portal or this site, please tell us.
        </p>
        <div class="split">
            <div>
                <h3>How to report</h3>
                <p>
                    Email <a href={`mailto:${security}`}>{security}</a> with &ldquo;Security&rdquo; in the
                    subject. Include the product and version, what you found, how to reproduce it, and
                    how we can reach you. The same contact is published in this site&rsquo;s
                    <a href="/.well-known/security.txt"><code>/.well-known/security.txt</code></a>.
                </p>
                <h3>What to expect</h3>
                <p>
                    We aim to acknowledge every report within three business days, keep you informed while
                    we investigate, and tell you when a fix ships. Security fixes reach customers as
                    releases through the licensing portal&rsquo;s Downloads page.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div>
                <h3>What we ask</h3>
                <ul class="ticks">
                    <li>Give us a reasonable time to fix the problem before you publish it</li>
                    <li>Test only against installations you are authorized to test, never against another customer&rsquo;s</li>
                    <li>Do not access, change or keep data that is not yours beyond what is needed to show the problem</li>
                    <li>Do not send us personal data from a live system; a description is enough</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
</BaseLayout>
